From aa5dc8dbd250aab03d95af447beed49b6b1d66fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 20:42:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add a parameter sweep for the fODF deconvolution
 regularization

The regularization weights suggested in the documentation were plausible
round numbers, not measured ones. This adds the simulation that measures
them and updates the documentation with its result.

example_fODF_regularization_sweep.m estimates the fODF of synthetic two
fiber voxels whose ground truth plm are known exactly, over crossing
angles of 40, 60 and 90 degrees and SNR of 20, 30 and 50 with 30 noise
realizations each, and scores the estimate against that ground truth.
The sweep is staged: lambda_nonneg against lambda_tikhonov jointly, then
tau, then Lmax_init, then the two Tikhonov matrices. The score minimized
is the relative L2 error of the fODF over the sphere, reported together
with the RMSE of the plm, the negative mass, and the mean angle between
the true fiber directions and the closest peak of the estimate, which is
the quantity tractography consumes.

All the deconvolutions of one parameter setting are batched into a
single call to get_plm_from_S_and_kernel by stacking the noise
realizations along the first image dimension. That is about three times
faster than one call per realization and gives identical results.

The result is that the non-negativity weight wants to be considerably
larger than the 1 that was suggested: the error falls monotonically from
lambda_nonneg 1 to 10 and rises again after it (0.105 at 10, 0.133 at
30, 0.417 at 100), so 10 is an interior optimum. tau = 0.1 and
Lmax_init = 4 are confirmed. The Laplace-Beltrami matrix is slightly
better than the identity once its weight is re-optimized. Against the
previously suggested settings the relative fODF error drops from 0.199
to 0.103 and the negative mass from 0.031 to 0.004.

The scoring is a separate file, fODF_regularization_score.m, rather than
a local function, so that the sweep also runs in GNU Octave, which
cannot call functions defined at the end of a script.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DPJMgJKrAuvxjsXupa1WpJ
